35,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"The closeout files are quite revealing. Catalogue-style awards still draw users a decade later, while mechanism-focused ones often end with questions unresolved.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The phenotyping technicians are gone, but the glasshouse crew remains. We must fund projects that utilize the hands we actually have, not the ones we lost.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"The repository turnaround is now weeks, not years. That removes a major bottleneck for data-heavy proposals.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The computing agreement is generous, but animal-unit rates spiked. I need to know if the agency absorbs that cost or passes it to the awardee.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I found the spectroscopy suite idle for eleven weeks due to a lapsed contract. Also, breeding colony projects overrun schedules twice as often as computational ones.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"Midori, that aligns with my findings. We should favor proposals where the work can actually be completed, rather than those that simply stall.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"Midori, your data confirms my concern. We must prioritize proposals relying on the stable glasshouse crew, avoiding those needing the departed phenotyping staff.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, the glasshouse is booked solid for two cycles. We cannot assume spare capacity there. We must verify actual availability before committing.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, if the glasshouse is full, the subsidy is moot. I need to know if the agency covers the animal-unit rate hike or if the awardee eats it.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Ryuji, I worry about over-relying on the glasshouse. @Benjamin, please clarify if the agency covers those animal-unit costs.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Benjamin, I worry about hidden costs. @Midori, your schedule data is very helpful. We must ensure the work can actually finish.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Victoria, if the glasshouse is full, we must look elsewhere. @Benjamin, please clarify the animal-unit cost burden.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Victoria"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Benjamin, the animal facility gained veterinary cover, so capacity exists. @Midori, the repository speedup favors data-heavy work.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, @Midori, the agreement covers computing, not vivariums. The agency absorbs bench fees, but animal-unit hikes land squarely on the awardee.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, that is a heavy burden. @Ryuji, perhaps we should favor computational proposals to avoid these unexpected costs and schedule delays?","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with Midori. The closeout files show computational work finishes reliably, while biological projects often stall. Let us favor the stable path.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,I hesitate. The modelling analysts are seconded abroad until autumn. Computational work lacks the senior expertise to execute now. We must wait for their return.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"The repository speedup is a genuine advantage. I support computational proposals, provided the data infrastructure is robust.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,I concur. The cost structure favors computational work. We should prioritize proposals that avoid the animal-unit financial trap and schedule risks.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,I agree. Computational work seems safer and more reliable. Let’s ensure we pick a proposal that fits our current resources and avoids these pitfalls.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
